<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Marionette.Integration</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Marionette.NET.Integration</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!--
      Phase 1.5: End-to-End Eval Cases per MASTERPLAN Phase 1 deliverable.

      Each [Fact] spawns a fresh Sample.Wpf.TodoApp.exe child in headless MCP
      mode and drives it through stdio JSON-RPC. The fixture lifecycle
      ensures no orphan processes survive a test failure.

      We deliberately do NOT reference the runtime, source generator, or
      sample projects directly. Tests must exercise the full external
      contract, not internal APIs. The TodoApp exe path is resolved
      relative to this project at runtime.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="xunit.runner.json">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Phase 3.3 parallel-build fix: we previously registered a BeforeBuild
    target that called `dotnet build` on the TodoApp sample. Two parallel
    builders (the test project's BeforeBuild + the sample's own ordinary
    build during `dotnet build Marionette.NET.sln`) raced over the sample's
    `obj/` directory and intermittently failed with file-locked errors
    (PHASE2_FINDINGS follow-up #1).

    Fix: drop the BeforeBuild target. `TodoAppFixture` invokes
    `dotnet build` lazily on first use (with a static lock + `bool _built`
    flag so the build runs at most once per test session). The same
    EnableMcpAutomation=true override is passed through, so the fixture
    behaviour is identical from the tests' perspective. Solution-level
    builds now drive the sample build via the regular MSBuild graph
    rather than racing against it.

    We do NOT use a regular ProjectReference because:
      1. ProjectReference on a WinExe pulls assembly outputs into our
         net10.0 test project, which fails because the TodoApp targets
         net10.0-windows.
      2. We need the TodoApp built with EnableMcpAutomation=true; a plain
         reference inherits the test project's properties (Configuration,
         Platform) which leaves EnableMcpAutomation unset.
  -->

</Project>
